---
// /learn/frameql-an-introduction — hosts the deposited **v2.1** source (2026-08-16), byte-identical to
// the Zenodo record: sha256 4e50e315…04b49, 27,251 bytes, verified against
// zenodo.org/api/records/21966453 rather than against the filename. Title/subtitle come from the
// deposit's own front-matter; the DOI and edition line below are page CHROME, so nothing is injected
// into the hosted markdown. Middle rung of the FrameQL ladder: /what-is-frameql (5 min) → this
// introduction (25 min) → the Manual (/docs/frameql).
//
// RE-EDITIONED v1.1 → v2.1 (Huayin's ruling, 2026-08-23). The registry's current record for
// `w-frameql-introduction` had been `.r05` (v2.1, 21966453) since 2026-08-16 while this route went on
// rendering `.r02` (v1.1, 21774643) — four deposits behind, and the site named v2.1 on no surface at
// all, so no reader could reach it. The route is a byte-faithful deposited-edition surface, which
// means the repair is a REPOINT (the ledger's own word) and never an edit: same URL, new edition,
// v1.1 retained on disk and unimported exactly as `launch_story_v6` and the 6e/6f manual editions are.
// The prior swap of this same file (v1 → v1.1, commit f27318d, the ToD v4.0 measure migration) has the
// identical shape.
//
// WHAT THE NEW EDITION CHANGES, in one line, because it is the reason the swap matters: v1.1 teaches
// the ToD v4 ontology — `measure` IS the family, `member` is its realization — and v2.1 teaches ToD
// v6, where `measure = measure family @ anchor`. The same word names different objects in the two
// texts. The Manual remains normative for shipped syntax and behaviour; this route is the conceptual
// on-ramp, not the reference.
//
// The ```frameql fences fall back to plaintext under the site's Shiki (no highlighter error). The
// deposit's LaTeX — eleven `$$…$$` blocks and the inline `$F@A$` — renders through the site's math
// pipeline (astro.config.mjs, PR #212); none of it required touching these frozen bytes.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { Content, frontmatter } from '../../content/corpus/frameql_an_introduction_v2_1.md';
const DOI = '10.5281/zenodo.21966453';
const EDITION = 'Version 2.1 · 16 August 2026';
---
<BaseLayout title="Frame-QL: An Introduction — datumwise"
  description="The introduction to Frame-QL — the output frame is the query. Why rows, tables, joins, and GROUP BY are not primitives of the request interface, and where AI belongs at the intent boundary.">
  <div class="page-head">
    <p class="kicker"><a href="/learn">Learn</a></p>
    <h1 class="serif">{frontmatter.title}</h1>
    <p class="lede">{frontmatter.subtitle}</p>
    <p class="doi mono">{EDITION} · <a href={`https://doi.org/${DOI}`} rel="noopener" target="_blank">doi.org/{DOI}</a></p>
  </div>

  <article class="prose">
    <Content />
  </article>

  <p class="evidence-note">
    <strong>This edition.</strong> The page above is <em>Frame-QL: An Introduction</em>,
    <strong>Version 2.1</strong>, deposited <strong>16 August 2026</strong>, reproduced from the
    deposit byte for byte. It supersedes the Version 1.1 edition previously served at this URL, whose
    record — like every earlier one — remains in the publication registry. The Frame-QL Manual stays
    authoritative for shipped syntax and behaviour; this is the conceptual on-ramp, not the reference.
  </p>

  <p class="engage wayfinding">
    Lighter first: <a href="/what-is-frameql">What is FrameQL — and why?</a> ·
    The reference, complete: <a href="/docs/frameql">the FrameQL Manual</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-head { width: min(100% - 3rem, var(--measure)); margin: 0 auto; }
  .page-head h1 { font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 4vw, 2.6rem); margin: 0.3rem 0 0.5rem; }
  .kicker a { color: var(--ink-faint); text-decoration: none; }
  .kicker a:hover { color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .lede { font-size: 1.05rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .doi { font-size: 0.8rem; margin-top: 0.4rem; }
  .doi a { color: var(--ink-faint); }
  .evidence-note { width: min(100% - 3rem, var(--measure)); margin: 3rem auto 0; padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .engage { width: min(100% - 3rem, var(--measure)); margin: 1.5rem auto 0;
    font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
</style>
